{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>לֹא תֹאבֶה לוֹ</b> — you shall not be desirous of him, and you shall not love him, since it is stated (Leviticus 19:18) \"and you shall love your fellow as yourself\" — this one you shall not love. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 13:9), from Sifrei (see 67). But in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 61a) they said: if he was willing and listened, he is liable. And so indeed is the matter, for אבה is an expression of willingness: \"and YHWH your Elohim was not willing (לֹא אָבָה) to listen to Balaam\" (Deuteronomy 23:6) — He did not wish to; \"my brother-in-law refuses (לֹא אָבָה יַבְמִי)\" (Deuteronomy 25:7) — from the fact that it says (ibid.) \"and if he does not desire (וְאִם לֹא יַחְפֹּז)\". Now, לֹא תֹאבֶה לוֹ — in the heart, and לֹא תִשְׁמַע אֵלָיו — that you acknowledge to him, saying, \"Yes, I will do so; I will go and worship idolatry as you have said,\" and he is liable to death by virtue of his admission. And so is the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 13:9): לָא תְקַבֵּל מִנֵּהּ (you shall not accept from him). And these negative commandments are like וְלֹא תִתְאַוֶּה and וְלֹא תַחְמֹד (Deuteronomy 5:18): if he coveted in his heart, he transgresses the first prohibition, and if he proceeded to act upon what he intended, he also transgresses the second prohibition." "<b>וְלֹא תָחוֹס עֵינְךָ עָלָיו</b> — to have pity on him, so that he not be executed." "<b>וְלֹא תַחְמֹל</b> — upon the closeness that is mentioned, which he has with you, as in the matter stated (Malachi 3:17) \"as a man has compassion upon his son who serves him.\"" "<b>וְלֹא תְכַסֵּה עָלָיו</b> — from testifying against him in a court of law. And in the Sifrei (see 68): לֹא תַחְמֹל — you shall not argue merit on his behalf; וְלֹא תְכַסֵּה — if you know of liability against him, you are not permitted to remain silent. And all of it is one rationale, for כִּסּוּי (covering) is from מְכַסֶּה פְּשָׁעָיו (Proverbs 28:13), and וְכוֹסֶה קָלוֹן (Proverbs 12:16) — silence from telling. And Scripture says, וְלֹא יֹסִיפוּ לַעֲשׂוֹת כַּדָּבָר הָרָע הַזֶּה, for it is a common thing, done constantly, were it not for the punishment, but the matter of the prophet mentioned above is not a frequent occurrence."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The verse cited as לא יחפוץ in Deuteronomy 25:7 actually reads לא חפץ קום in most editions; Ramban may have cited loosely or followed a variant reading.", 3 "Ramban appears to contrast the closing verse of this passage (Deuteronomy 13:12, regarding the inciter) with the case of the false prophet discussed earlier in the chapter; the reference to 'the prophet mentioned above' points back to verses 13:2–6."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:24.850898216Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}